"""Editorial question designs for mystery, romance, and comedy stories."""

from review_angle_plans import audit, revise_domain


MYSTERY = """history_origins|A Detective Form Develops|Which dated publication changed how clues and inquiry were narrated?|Compare original texts and publishing context without a single-origin claim.
famous_examples|A Famous Reveal Rechecked|Were the clues for a celebrated solution actually available to readers?|List each clue by page and time of disclosure.
hidden_stories|Investigators Beyond the Canon|Which overlooked narrators or writers changed mystery fiction?|Use dated editions, archives, and reception.
beginner_explainers|Question, Clue, Solution|How does a mystery make its central question answerable?|Map evidence, inference, and final explanation in one short work.
advanced_concepts|Unreliable Evidence in a Mystery|How does narrator access alter what counts as a clue?|Compare what narrator, investigator, and reader know.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|A Mystery Left Open by Design|Which question does a work deliberately leave unresolved?|Distinguish textual ambiguity from missing pages or editions.
technology_innovation|Forensics in Fiction|What real technique does a mystery depict, and where does it fictionalize it?|Compare the text with dated primary forensic guidance.
people_personalities|An Author's Solution Revised|What evidence explains a changed culprit or clue?|Use drafts and attributed editorial records.
major_events|A Mystery's Publication Turning Point|How did serialization, translation, or adaptation change a work's reach?|Trace dated versions and documented readers.
how_it_works|Reasoning from Clues|Which inference connects an early clue to the solution?|Test alternative explanations using the same evidence.
rise_fall|A Solution Reassessed|Why did later readers challenge a once-accepted ending?|Compare dated criticism and versions.
then_vs_now|Two Eras of Investigation|How do institutional roles and privacy assumptions change across mysteries?|Compare bounded works and publication contexts.
future_developments|A New Mystery Format|What changes when an audience can choose what evidence to inspect?|Analyze a released interactive work and its paths.
myths_vs_facts|The Perfectly Fair Puzzle?|Which information is withheld despite a work's fair-play reputation?|Define the rule and audit clue timing.
documentary_stories|A Mystery's Draft Trail|How did an author plant and revise the solution?|Follow dated outlines, drafts, and final text.
creative_brief|Designing the Central Unknown|Which question must a mystery sustain until its reveal?|Compare pitch, outline, and finished work.
materials_and_tools|Clue Lists and Timelines|How do planning notes keep evidence and alibis consistent?|Use documented artifacts and their version dates.
process_breakdown|Planting a Clue|How was a clue introduced, repeated, and reinterpreted?|Compare draft passages and final payoff.
composition_choice|A Scene with Two Meanings|What does a reader notice before and after the solution?|Close-read a bounded passage without invented intent.
collaborative_authorship|Building an Adapted Mystery|How did writer, editor, performer, and director shape the reveal?|Compare credited drafts and final scenes.
adaptation_across_media|A Clue Moves to Screen|What changes when a written clue becomes image or sound?|Compare source, script, and cut; track spoiler timing.
audience_reception|Did Readers Solve It?|What actual responses document how readers interpreted clues?|Use dated, situated feedback instead of assuming surprise.
cultural_context|Law and Investigation in Fiction|Which real institutional assumptions does a mystery use or challenge?|Compare work with dated jurisdictional context.
preservation_restoration|A Mystery Across Editions|Did cuts or translation alter a clue or ending?|Compare identified texts and archival provenance.
rights_and_attribution|A Detective Character's Versions|Who created particular character traits in text and adaptation?|Separate source credit, later additions, and rights.
style_comparison|Two Ways to Hide a Clue|How do viewpoint and scene placement change reader inference?|Compare matched narrative functions.
creative_failure|A Reveal Rewritten|Which documented problem led to a changed solution?|Use editorial notes or draft differences, not guessed audience reaction.
field_internal_rules|Fair Clue Access|Does the solution require information withheld from the reader?|Inventory available evidence before reveal.
field_character_motivation|Why Conceal the Truth?|Do the suspect's actions follow established incentives and knowledge?|Trace chronology, alternatives, and contradictions.
field_narrative_pacing|Clues Between Reveals|How does spacing evidence alter suspense without breaking logic?|Map clue intervals and reader knowledge.
field_setting_logic|An Alibi in Place and Time|Could the stated movement occur within the story's geography?|Check distances, timestamps, and scene continuity.
field_genre_expectations|A Mystery That Refuses Closure|Which expected solution does a work decline to provide?|Compare framing, text, and documented reception.
"""

ROMANCE = """history_origins|A Love-Story Convention Changes|Which dated publishing or performance context shaped a romance form?|Compare works and audience context without one origin story.
famous_examples|A Celebrated Relationship Arc|What choices make a named couple's change believable in the text?|Trace decisions, dialogue, obstacles, and revisions.
hidden_stories|Readers and Writers Missing from the Canon|Whose romance traditions were left out of a standard account?|Use attributed works and publication records.
beginner_explainers|The Central Relationship|How does a romance keep the relationship central alongside other plots?|Map scenes and consequences in one work.
advanced_concepts|Love, Power, and Choice|How do asymmetries of status or resources shape consent and agency?|Trace character options without diagnosing people.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|An Ambiguous Ending|Does a story establish a continuing relationship or leave it open?|Read ending, earlier promises, and alternate editions.
technology_innovation|Romance in a New Medium|How does serial or interactive delivery alter relationship pacing?|Compare released formats and audience choices.
people_personalities|A Writer's Relationship Revision|Why did a creator change a character's choice?|Use drafts and attributed accounts, not speculation.
major_events|A Romance Publication Milestone|Which dated release changed access to a particular love story?|Trace editions, translation, adaptation, and reception.
how_it_works|A Relationship Changes|Which interaction changes what two characters know or want?|Trace reciprocal action and consequence scene by scene.
rise_fall|A Romance Trope Reassessed|Why did readers' interpretation of one trope change?|Compare dated works and situated criticism.
then_vs_now|Two Love Stories, Different Norms|How do two period-specific works frame marriage, work, or autonomy?|Compare actual choices within historical context.
future_developments|A New Romance Format|What reader agency does an emerging format actually allow?|Analyze a released work rather than predict the genre.
myths_vs_facts|One Required Romance Ending?|Which subgenres or adjacent love stories use different resolution rules?|Define the publishing category and compare examples.
documentary_stories|Making a Relationship Arc|How did draft and editorial changes reshape a couple?|Follow versioned scenes and creator accounts.
creative_brief|What Relationship Is Promised?|What emotional trajectory did a specific brief or pitch propose?|Compare brief, revisions, and finished story.
materials_and_tools|Character Notes and Scene Maps|How do planning artifacts track two perspectives?|Check dates, authorship, and final text.
process_breakdown|Revising a Turning Point|Which change made a choice clearer or more consequential?|Compare drafts and subsequent scenes.
composition_choice|A Conversation with Subtext|What do gesture, silence, and dialogue reveal about a character's choice?|Close-read a bounded scene and alternatives.
collaborative_authorship|An Adapted Love Story|How did author, editor, performers, and director shape its central relationship?|Use credits and documented version history.
adaptation_across_media|Intimacy from Page to Screen|Which inner thoughts become action, voice, or omission?|Compare source passage, script, and finished scene.
audience_reception|Different Readers, One Couple|Which responses to the relationship are documented?|Use situated reviews and avoid universal approval claims.
cultural_context|Love under Local Constraints|How do family, law, language, or class shape this particular relationship?|Use work and period sources without generalizing a community.
preservation_restoration|A Romance across Editions|Which translation or edit changed a relationship's meaning?|Compare identified versions and translator choices.
rights_and_attribution|Whose Love Story Is Retold?|Which elements belong to source author, adapter, or performer?|Trace credits, agreements, and visible changes.
style_comparison|Two Courtship Scenes|How do point of view and pacing change perceived intimacy?|Compare matched scenes and context.
creative_failure|A Relationship Beat Removed|What documented concern caused a scene to be cut?|Use drafts or editorial notes rather than assumed chemistry.
field_internal_rules|Promises and Payoffs|Does an ending honor the relationship possibilities established earlier?|Track stated goals, changed choices, and resolution.
field_character_motivation|Why Choose This Partner?|How do a character's reasons change through reciprocal encounters?|Trace actions and credible alternatives.
field_narrative_pacing|Distance between Turning Points|How does scene spacing affect trust or reconciliation?|Map conflict, reflection, and changed behavior.
field_setting_logic|Social Rules around the Couple|Which specific setting constraint keeps them apart or together?|Check place, period, and internal consistency.
field_genre_expectations|The Romance Contract|Does a work invite an optimistic ending or another love-story mode?|Compare text and publisher framing; state category used.
"""

COMEDY = """history_origins|A Comic Form in Context|Which dated performance or publishing practice shaped a particular joke form?|Trace versions and local audience context.
famous_examples|A Comic Scene Revisited|How does setup, expectation, and turn work in a named scene?|Analyze exact beats, delivery, and audience information.
hidden_stories|Collaborators Behind a Joke|Whose writing, performance, or edit shaped a familiar comic moment?|Check drafts, credits, rehearsals, and production records.
beginner_explainers|What Makes a Setup Pay Off?|Which expectation does a joke establish and break?|Map a short example without claiming universal laughter.
advanced_concepts|Satire's Target|Who or what does a specific joke invite the audience to criticize?|Compare wording, context, and documented readings.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|A Joke with Disputed Origin|Which attribution can surviving sources support?|Date performances, scripts, recordings, and recollections.
technology_innovation|Comic Timing in a New Format|How did recording or editing change one gag's delivery?|Compare performed, filmed, or clipped versions.
people_personalities|A Performer's Comic Choice|Which documented change in timing or gesture changed a scene?|Use rehearsals, takes, or attributed accounts.
major_events|A Comic Work Meets Its Audience|What happened when one show or story crossed a new market?|Use dated release and situated response.
how_it_works|The Three-Beat Gag|How does repetition establish a rule before the payoff?|Track each beat and explain the reversal.
rise_fall|A Joke Ages Unevenly|How did responses to one work change across periods?|Compare dated criticism with social context.
then_vs_now|The Same Gag in Two Media|What changes between stage, film, and short online clip?|Compare timing, audience feedback, and edit.
future_developments|Comedy in an Interactive Format|Which released format lets an audience alter timing or outcome?|Analyze actual paths and constraints.
myths_vs_facts|Everyone Finds This Funny?|What do documented audience responses show and leave unknown?|Specify sample and avoid universal taste claims.
documentary_stories|Making a Comic Scene|How did draft, rehearsal, performance, and edit create its payoff?|Follow dated artifacts and collaborators.
creative_brief|What Is the Joke Trying to Do?|Which audience or narrative function did a brief identify?|Compare stated aim with finished scene and response.
materials_and_tools|Props and Comic Business|How does one physical object enable a sequence of surprises?|Document blocking, continuity, and versions.
process_breakdown|A Punchline Rewritten|Which revision changes the expectation or target?|Compare draft lines and delivery context.
composition_choice|When to Show the Reaction|How does framing or point of view change a comic beat?|Compare shots or text order in a bounded scene.
collaborative_authorship|A Joke Built Together|What can be attributed to writer, performer, director, and editor?|Use versioned scripts and credits.
adaptation_across_media|A Written Gag on Screen|Which wordplay survives or becomes visual action?|Compare source, script, performance, and cut.
audience_reception|Laughter in Context|What is documented about different audience responses?|Distinguish recording, review, and assumed reaction.
cultural_context|A Joke across Languages|What changes when wordplay or social reference is translated?|Compare versions and local readings.
preservation_restoration|A Comedy Cut or Restored|Does a missing beat change setup or payoff?|Compare identified editions and archival evidence.
rights_and_attribution|Who Created the Routine?|What records establish authorship or license of a comic sequence?|Separate shared trope from a specific written or performed version.
style_comparison|Deadpan and Exaggeration|How do two performances deliver the same comic premise?|Compare timing, gesture, and audience context.
creative_failure|A Gag That Was Dropped|What documented feedback or constraint led to its removal?|Use drafts and production records, not guessed laughter.
field_internal_rules|A Comic Rule Repeated|Does a running gag retain enough pattern for its final variation?|Map repetition, exception, and payoff.
field_character_motivation|The Character Behind the Joke|Does a funny choice follow the character's goal and knowledge?|Trace incentives, mistake, and consequence.
field_narrative_pacing|The Pause before the Punchline|How does beat duration change the reveal?|Compare available versions and delivery conditions.
field_setting_logic|Where the Gag Can Happen|Does blocking or story geography support the comic surprise?|Check entrances, props, timing, and continuity.
field_genre_expectations|A Comedy Turns Serious|How does a work prepare viewers for a tonal shift?|Compare earlier cues, turn, and documented responses.
"""


if __name__ == "__main__":
    revise_domain("376", "376_mystery_fiction", MYSTERY,
                  "Mystery fiction as a controlled distribution of questions, clues, inferences, and solutions across reader and character viewpoints.",
                  "identified editions, dated drafts and outlines, clue inventories, publication and adaptation records, relevant historical sources, and situated reader responses",
                  "Keep the mystery and its solution specific to a work and edition. Distinguish literary clue logic from real forensic reliability and documented authorship from later lore.")
    revise_domain("377", "377_romance_stories", ROMANCE,
                  "Romance stories as narratives of a central relationship, its choices and constraints, and a resolution interpreted within a specified publishing category.",
                  "identified texts and editions, creator drafts, publisher framing, period context, adaptation records, and situated reader criticism",
                  "State whether the work is genre romance or an adjacent love story. Describe fictional relationships and agency without applying one ending rule to every cultural tradition.")
    revise_domain("378", "378_comedy_stories", COMEDY,
                  "Comedy stories as situated craft involving setup, timing, surprise, performance, audience expectations, and varying responses.",
                  "identified scripts and recordings, drafts, rehearsal and production records, dated reviews, and bounded audience evidence",
                  "Describe observable comic design and documented reception. Do not claim universal laughter, invent an origin for a joke, or overlook local context and attribution.")
    rows = audit()
    print(f"Plans: {len(rows)}; reviewed question designs: {sum(r['status'] == 'editorial_question_reviewed' for r in rows)}")
